<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Maghreb</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Maghrebi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Maghreb is the region of north-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> comprising present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a>. It was the heartland of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad Caliphate</a>, which unified it under one rule in the twelfth century. The Maghreb was linked by trans-Saharan trade to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songhai-empire">Songhai</a> empires of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maghreb.jpg" alt="Maghreb" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Connormah · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maghreb_(orthographic_projection).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
